<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johannes Brenz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1499–1570</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johannes Brenz was a Lutheran reformer and theologian who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in the imperial city of Schwäbisch Hall from 1522. He later reorganised the church of the Duchy of Württemberg and wrote a widely used catechism and church ordinances that shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He spent his last years as provost in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johannes-brenz.jpg" alt="Johannes Brenz" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann-Brenz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
